# Unity best practices

## Performance

- Prefer object pooling for any object that is spawned and destroyed frequently.
- Avoid garbage allocations in Update/FixedUpdate; reuse collections, avoid boxing.
- Cache references (transforms, components) in Awake/Start.
- Use Burst-compatible math (Unity.Mathematics) where heavy per-frame math is needed (see techContext).

## Architecture

- ScriptableObjects for data: weapon stats, item definitions, biome configs, creature part defs.
- Events over polling: use EventBus (Subscribe/Publish) for stage transitions, damage, spawns, UI updates.
- Keep systems small and single-purpose; folder structure: Core, Systems, Data, UI, Editor (see systemPatterns.md).

## Gameplay code

- No FindObjectOfType in gameplay loops; resolve at init or via events.
- Heavy logic in dedicated systems or coroutines, not in Update.
- Prefer RequireComponent and SerializeField for dependencies and inspector config.
